{% comment %}
  Selection logic only -- no layout, no styling. Drop this at the top of
  your markup and everything below it can use `pick` and `card_image`.

  It resolves three settings -- Orientation, Country, Era -- against
  today.json:

    several chosen  -> a card matching one value from each axis the
                       reader set, rotating a day at a time over the
                       combinations that exist
    some chosen     -> the axes they left alone are not constrained
    none chosen     -> today's card from the whole catalogue

  That last line is the rule the whole thing is built around: an
  untouched settings panel means the full catalogue, never an empty
  screen.

  `day_index` is the day number the schedule turns on, so the rotation
  holds all day and moves on by itself at midnight UTC.

  Two things this is careful about, both found by running it rather than
  reading it: a settings keyname and a feed key of the same name collide
  (hence orientation_options/country_options/era_options in the feed),
  and `split` leaves a trailing empty string on some engines, which is
  why nothing here indexes straight into a split array.
{% endcomment %}

{% assign settings = trmnl.plugin_settings.custom_fields_values %}
{% assign SEP = "__" %}

{% comment %}
  A multi-select arrives as an array, a single choice as a string, and
  an untouched field as nothing at all. Normalise all three to a list.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign chosen_orientation = settings.orientation %}
{% if chosen_orientation == nil %}{% assign chosen_orientation = "" | split: "," %}{% endif %}
{% if chosen_orientation.first == nil %}{% assign chosen_orientation = chosen_orientation | split: "," %}{% endif %}

{% assign chosen_country = settings.country %}
{% if chosen_country == nil %}{% assign chosen_country = "" | split: "," %}{% endif %}
{% if chosen_country.first == nil %}{% assign chosen_country = chosen_country | split: "," %}{% endif %}

{% assign chosen_era = settings.era %}
{% if chosen_era == nil %}{% assign chosen_era = "" | split: "," %}{% endif %}
{% if chosen_era.first == nil %}{% assign chosen_era = chosen_era | split: "," %}{% endif %}

{% comment %}
  A boolean field arrives as the STRING "true" or "false", so it has to
  be compared, not just tested -- {% raw %}{% if show_caption %}{% endraw %} is true even when the
  reader turned it off. An untouched field arrives as nothing, so the
  default matters: the caption is on unless switched off, the credit off
  unless switched on. A fresh install shows a captioned card.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign want_caption = true %}
{% if settings.show_caption == "false" %}{% assign want_caption = false %}{% endif %}

{% assign want_place = true %}
{% if settings.show_place == "false" %}{% assign want_place = false %}{% endif %}

{% assign want_credit = false %}
{% if settings.show_credit == "true" %}{% assign want_credit = true %}{% endif %}

{% comment %}
  What the reader saw -> the keys this feed uses. keys_by_label carries
  every spelling a setting might come back as, because TRMNL sends a
  value derived from the label rather than the label itself.

  Each axis ends up as a comma-terminated string. An axis nobody set
  becomes "all", which constrains nothing -- that is what makes an
  untouched panel mean the whole catalogue.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign o_keys = "" %}
{% for label in chosen_orientation %}
  {% assign lbl = label | strip %}
  {% assign k = keys_by_label[lbl] %}
  {% if k %}{% assign o_keys = o_keys | append: k | append: "," %}{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% if o_keys == "" %}{% assign o_keys = "all," %}{% endif %}

{% assign c_keys = "" %}
{% for label in chosen_country %}
  {% assign lbl = label | strip %}
  {% assign k = keys_by_label[lbl] %}
  {% if k %}{% assign c_keys = c_keys | append: k | append: "," %}{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% if c_keys == "" %}{% assign c_keys = "all," %}{% endif %}

{% assign e_keys = "" %}
{% for label in chosen_era %}
  {% assign lbl = label | strip %}
  {% assign k = keys_by_label[lbl] %}
  {% if k %}{% assign e_keys = e_keys | append: k | append: "," %}{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% if e_keys == "" %}{% assign e_keys = "all," %}{% endif %}

{% assign o_list = o_keys | split: "," %}
{% assign c_list = c_keys | split: "," %}
{% assign e_list = e_keys | split: "," %}

{% comment %} Every combination the reader asked for that the feed actually carries. {% endcomment %}
{% assign wanted = "" %}
{% for o in o_list %}{% if o != "" %}
  {% for c in c_list %}{% if c != "" %}
    {% for e in e_list %}{% if e != "" %}
      {% capture cell %}{{ o }}{{ SEP }}{{ c }}{{ SEP }}{{ e }}{% endcapture %}
      {% if cell_keys contains cell %}
        {% assign wanted = wanted | append: cell | append: "," %}
      {% endif %}
    {% endif %}{% endfor %}
  {% endif %}{% endfor %}
{% endif %}{% endfor %}

{% comment %}
  Nothing matched, so loosen one axis at a time rather than dropping
  straight to the whole catalogue. Someone asking for portrait cards
  from Morocco before 1900 has asked for something thin on the ground,
  and should still get a card from Morocco.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign n = 0 %}
{% assign wanted_list = wanted | split: "," %}
{% for k in wanted_list %}{% if k != "" %}{% assign n = n | plus: 1 %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}

{% if n == 0 %}
  {% for o in o_list %}{% if o != "" %}
    {% for c in c_list %}{% if c != "" %}
      {% capture cell %}{{ o }}{{ SEP }}{{ c }}{{ SEP }}all{% endcapture %}
      {% if cell_keys contains cell %}{% assign wanted = wanted | append: cell | append: "," %}{% endif %}
    {% endif %}{% endfor %}
  {% endif %}{% endfor %}
  {% assign wanted_list = wanted | split: "," %}
  {% for k in wanted_list %}{% if k != "" %}{% assign n = n | plus: 1 %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}
{% endif %}

{% if n == 0 %}
  {% for c in c_list %}{% if c != "" %}
    {% capture cell %}all{{ SEP }}{{ c }}{{ SEP }}all{% endcapture %}
    {% if cell_keys contains cell %}{% assign wanted = wanted | append: cell | append: "," %}{% endif %}
  {% endif %}{% endfor %}
  {% assign wanted_list = wanted | split: "," %}
  {% for k in wanted_list %}{% if k != "" %}{% assign n = n | plus: 1 %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}
{% endif %}

{% if n == 0 %}
  {% for e in e_list %}{% if e != "" %}
    {% capture cell %}all{{ SEP }}all{{ SEP }}{{ e }}{% endcapture %}
    {% if cell_keys contains cell %}{% assign wanted = wanted | append: cell | append: "," %}{% endif %}
  {% endif %}{% endfor %}
  {% assign wanted_list = wanted | split: "," %}
  {% for k in wanted_list %}{% if k != "" %}{% assign n = n | plus: 1 %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}
{% endif %}

{% if n == 0 %}
  {% assign wanted_list = default | split: "," %}
  {% assign n = 1 %}
{% endif %}

{% comment %} Today's cell: the nth non-empty entry, n rotating by day. {% endcomment %}
{% assign i = day_index | modulo: n %}
{% assign j = 0 %}
{% assign chosen_key = default %}
{% for k in wanted_list %}
  {% if k != "" %}
    {% if j == i %}{% assign chosen_key = k %}{% endif %}
    {% assign j = j | plus: 1 %}
  {% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% assign pick = picks[chosen_key] %}
{% if pick == nil %}{% assign pick = picks[default] %}{% endif %}

{% comment %}
  pick.image is already sized for the largest panel there is (1872x1404,
  the X) and every smaller screen scales it down for free, so it is safe
  to use as-is. It asks for the colour scan rather than a greyscale one:
  a colour panel can then show the colour, and a monochrome one loses
  nothing -- converting the colour file to luminance lands within 1/255
  of the archive's own greyscale version.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign card_image = pick.image %}

{% comment %}
  From here on:
    card_image      the postcard, ready to use at any panel size
    pick.title      the card's own caption, trimmed to fit
    pick.date       "1907" or "1900-1906"
    pick.place      "Athens, Region of Attica, Greece"
    pick.country    "Greece"
    pick.publisher  who printed it, when the archive knows
    pick.credit     the holding institution and collection
    pick.rights     the rights statement
    pick.source_url the catalogue record
    want_caption    whether to draw the caption block at all
    want_place      whether that block includes the place line
    want_credit     whether to print the credit
{% endcomment %}
